      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded number of uploaded file parts allows resource-exhaustion DoS.\n\nThe multipart parser gates FIELD parts against settings.DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FIELDS, but the FILE branch (item_type == FILE) has no per-request limit on the number of file parts; each part invokes handler.new_file(), which for the default TemporaryFileUploadHandler creates a new on-disk temporary file. An unauthenticated attacker controls the number of parts via the request body.\n\nImpact: A single multipart request containing a very large number of small file parts causes creation of a correspondingly large number of temporary files and handler invocations, exhausting file descriptors, inodes, disk space, and CPU on the server, resulting in denial of service. There is no default Django-level cap on the number of file parts (no DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FILES setting exists in this version).\n\nExploit scenario: An attacker POSTs multipart/form-data to any view that reads request.POST/request.FILES (or triggers upload parsing). The body contains hundreds of thousands of minimal file parts (each ~70 bytes: a Content-Disposition with a filename and empty content). The parser calls new_file() for every part, spawning that many NamedTemporaryFile handles and consuming server resources until file descriptors/inodes/disk are exhausted.\n\nPreconditions: A request path that causes multipart parsing (any view accessing request.POST or request.FILES on a POST, or middleware doing so); Default or TemporaryFileUploadHandler in FILE_UPLOAD_HANDLERS; No front-end proxy imposing a strict request-body-size or part-count limit.\n\nRecommendation: Introduce and enforce a DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FILES limit in the FILE branch analogous to the DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FIELDS check, raising TooManyFilesSent when exceeded (this is the fix later shipped as CVE-2023-24580 mitigation).\n\nCWE-770. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "django/http/multipartparser.py",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 235,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "                                handler.new_file("
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "MultiPartParser.parse",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "MultiPartParser.parse"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F1",
              "title": "Unbounded number of uploaded file parts allows resource-exhaustion DoS",
              "impact": "A single multipart request containing a very large number of small file parts causes creation of a correspondingly large number of temporary files and handler invocations, exhausting file descriptors, inodes, disk space, and CPU on the server, resulting in denial of service. There is no default Django-level cap on the number of file parts (no DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FILES setting exists in this version).",
              "file": "django/http/multipartparser.py",
              "line": 235,
              "description": "The multipart parser gates FIELD parts against settings.DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FIELDS, but the FILE branch (item_type == FILE) has no per-request limit on the number of file parts; each part invokes handler.new_file(), which for the default TemporaryFileUploadHandler creates a new on-disk temporary file. An unauthenticated attacker controls the number of parts via the request body.",
              "exploit_scenario": "An attacker POSTs multipart/form-data to any view that reads request.POST/request.FILES (or triggers upload parsing). The body contains hundreds of thousands of minimal file parts (each ~70 bytes: a Content-Disposition with a filename and empty content). The parser calls new_file() for every part, spawning that many NamedTemporaryFile handles and consuming server resources until file descriptors/inodes/disk are exhausted.",
              "preconditions": [
                "A request path that causes multipart parsing (any view accessing request.POST or request.FILES on a POST, or middleware doing so)",
                "Default or TemporaryFileUploadHandler in FILE_UPLOAD_HANDLERS",
                "No front-end proxy imposing a strict request-body-size or part-count limit"
              ],
              "category": "Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "recommendation": "Introduce and enforce a DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FILES limit in the FILE branch analogous to the DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FIELDS check, raising TooManyFilesSent when exceeded (this is the fix later shipped as CVE-2023-24580 mitigation).",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-770",
              "snippet": "                                handler.new_file(",
              "symbol": "MultiPartParser.parse",
              "declared_line": 235,
              "claudeSecurityPluginFindingId": "v3:ae07d8dd9978bbe507fcf0e8148b78cc588ef460288b3d151c4dbf5970fc21b2",
